  Tea reached Europe in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, carried by Portuguese and Dutch traders who sailed to Asia. Early European reactions were
  mixed. Some physicians praised tea as a cure for many ailments, while others were suspicious of it. By the mid-seventeenth century, tea had become fashionable
  among the wealthy in the Netherlands, and it soon spread to England. The marriage of Catherine of Braganza to Charles II in 1662 is often cited as a moment
  that boosted tea's popularity among the English aristocracy, with the princess reputedly bringing the habit of tea drinking to the court. Over the following
  century, tea shifted from an elite luxury to a staple of the working population, and the British came to consume enormous quantities of it.

  This growing demand made tea a central commodity in global commerce, and it also made it a source of political conflict. The British East India Company
  dominated the trade with China, which was tightly controlled by the imperial government. Because Chinese merchants wanted silver rather than British goods in
  exchange for tea, the British sought other commodities to sell. Their solution was opium grown in British-controlled India, and the resulting addiction and
  economic drain provoked the Opium Wars of the 1839 to 1842 and 1856 to 1860 periods. The tea trade was therefore bound up with imperial coercion. Tax disputes
  over tea also fuelled the American colonial rebellion: the Tea Act of 1773 led to the Boston Tea Party, when colonists protesting taxation without
  representation dumped imported tea into the harbour.

  Perhaps the most dramatic chapter of tea's history is its transplantation outside China. For much of the nineteenth century, China held a near-monopoly on tea
  production, and Britain was determined to break it. In the 1820s, British officials discovered native tea plants growing in Assam, in northeastern India, and
  commercial cultivation began soon afterward. The more ambitious effort came from the botanist Robert Fortune, who in the late 1840s travelled into Chinese
  tea regions in disguise, collecting plants and seeds and studying processing methods. His expeditions helped establish tea gardens in the Himalayan foothills,
  including Darjeeling, and in Ceylon, where planters turned to tea after a coffee disease devastated their crops in the 1860s. These estates were built on the
  labour of workers, many of them migrants from southern India, who lived under harsh and often exploitative conditions. The modern tea industry of South Asia,
  and much of its social history, grew from this colonial system.

  The nineteenth century also saw the rise of new styles and new markets. Black tea, fully oxidised and robust, suited the British taste for strong brews with
  milk and sugar, and it became the dominant form in the West. Companies such as Lipton, founded in the late nineteenth century, sold tea at lower prices and in
  larger volumes, helping to make it a mass-market product. Innovations in packaging transformed everyday habits. The tea bag, often credited to the American
  merchant Thomas Sullivan around 1908, made brewing quicker and more convenient, though purists have long been sceptical of it. Iced tea is popularly linked to
  a heatwave at the 1904 St. Louis World's Fair, though the story is more legend than documented fact.

  In the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, tea has continued to change. Global production is now concentrated in China, India, Kenya, and Sri Lanka, and the
  industry faces challenges including climate change, labour disputes, and the pressures of a commodity market. At the same time, interest in specialty and
  single-origin teas has grown, and consumers increasingly value the complexity of green, white, oolong, and aged teas that the mass market once overlooked.
  Traditions that once seemed fragile, such as the Chinese gongfu style of brewing and the Japanese tea ceremony, have found new audiences around the world.

  Looking back across four thousand years, tea's history is a record of human ingenuity and human cost. It began as a medicinal leaf in the mountains of
  southwestern China, became a refined art in the courts and monasteries of Tang and Song China, and was reshaped by Ming innovations and Japanese devotion. It
  then became entangled with the empires that pursued it, spanning oceans, provoking wars, and building plantations on the backs of labourers. Yet the simple
  act of pouring hot water over leaves has endured, and a cup of tea remains one of the most widely shared rituals on earth.

